**The main finding: two of the site's refutations didn't hold up when checked.** The two visitor personas who read the physics closely both said the refutations were less carefully checked than the original claims had been. I checked the two they named before fixing anything else.

1. **"The compander form loses 2.10× to MOND's μ": withdrawn.** The comparison was described as changing only the function, but the code also changed the knee (0.32 a₀ instead of 0.5 a₀) and the way the floor is applied. MOND's μ was clipped at 0.089; the compander had 0.089 added underneath everywhere, which removes boost at every radius. I wrote down predictions and committed them before running:

   | Run | χ²/N | vs MOND |
   |---|---|---|
   | MOND μ | 51.45 | 1.00× |
   | Compander at γ = ½, algebraically identical to MOND (check) | 51.45 | 1.00× |
   | **Compander at γ = 0.489, only the function changed** | **50.83** | **0.99×, a tie** |
   | Knee change alone | 77.98 | 1.52× |
   | Floor change alone | 67.69 | 1.32× |
   | Both, as originally run | 108.10 | 2.10× (reproduced) |

   A 09-10 proposal awaiting your decision cited this number as the reason to reclassify TEST-25 as specific to this framework. I've withdrawn that recommendation in the research repo; TEST-25 stays "inherited from MOND", with the question open.

2. **"A density-keyed law has no external field to appeal to" holds only in the algebraic reading.** Under the field equation ∇·[C∇Φ] = 4πGρ, the Milky Way's field adds on and bends inside any cluster whose density crosses the knee. I ran a rough estimate for one typical cluster; it wasn't pre-registered as a test, but I wrote the expected size down first and it matched:
   - Beyond the knee, the uneven part of that field is 0.3–0.37 of the Galactic field strength.
   - That is 0.25–0.67 of the cluster's own gravity at 20–30 pc, and more than all of it by about 35–40 pc.

   The globular-cluster window is now labelled as holding for isolated clusters only. The effect cancels in averaged velocity-dispersion profiles, so its likely signature is where clusters get truncated. That could be a new test that applies to Refracted Gravity too, and I've queued it for the explorer as a high-priority topic. I also caught two bugs in my own estimate script; one had produced an exactly-zero result that was a solver artifact.

